WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:11.300
So I want to talk about some more attributes
of this structure.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:18.410
Okay, now, broadly speaking what we’re going
to say is that a motivation sets up one of

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:19.410
these frameworks.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:22.690
I’m going to call this framework a story.

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:25.340
Now there’s lots of things you could call
it.

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:29.920
You could call it an expectancy model, you
could call it a game; I’m going to call

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:34.420
it a story and we’re going to say well what
you do is you have a life story and your life

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:37.480
story is composed of nested sub-stories.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:41.379
You could also think of it as a personality,
but a story is like a personality in action.

00:00:41.379 --> 00:00:43.920
So, we’ll just stick with story.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:48.219
The story has a starting place, that’s where
you are, and the story has an endpoint, that’s

00:00:48.219 --> 00:00:49.219
where you’re going.

00:00:49.219 --> 00:00:52.550
And you’re always in one of those stories,
or you’re asleep.

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:59.570
So, you’re either pursuing something in
the real world that is of value, that’s

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:04.489
directly motivated, or you’re pursuing another
situation where the reason you’re pursing

00:01:04.489 --> 00:01:08.390
it is because it fulfills a bunch of motivational
states now and into the future.

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:11.510
So that ‘s what you’re doing when you
pursue, say a career.

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:17.300
And, if you’re not doing that, then your
mind is wandering and what it’s doing is

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:23.440
it’s wandering through the space of hypothetical
worlds where you can play hypothetical roles.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.610
And if you’re not doing that then you’re
asleep.

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:27.970
So you’re in one of these all the time.

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:32.280
Now they shift, and that’s one of the things
that’s quite interesting about them.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.870
The shift can take place on all sorts of levels.

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:39.960
You can differently conceptualize the way,
where you are.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:43.709
You can differently conceptualize where you’re
going, and you can differently conceptualize

00:01:43.709 --> 00:01:45.789
how you’re going to get there.

00:01:45.789 --> 00:01:54.110
Now, I won’t go into that for the moment.

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:59.150
So let’s say you’re going from point A
to point B. At the highest level of resolution

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:01.080
that means that you’re acting.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:05.180
And then other things that happen is not only
are you acting, you’re configuring your

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:06.180
perceptions.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:07.550
We already talked a little bit about this.

00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:11.350
You know what you should highlight, what you
should bring your high resolution thinking

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:14.220
to bear on, and you know what you should ignore.

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:18.420
And what you should ignore is those things
that are likely to remain constant as you

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:25.130
pursue the set of transformations, or that
aren’t going to interfere with what you’re

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:26.130
doing.

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:29.700
And so you just have to concentrate, especially
if you set your environment up properly, and

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:30.700
that’s another thing.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:34.850
Part of the way that people deal with the
complexity of the world is by just not having

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:36.250
it around.

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:40.470
You know so for example, even if you look
in this room, a lot of things are radically

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:43.010
simplified so that you don’t get overwhelmed.

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:45.190
“How many of you have computers here?

00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:48.280
Okay, now what does your computer look like?

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:49.420
Keyboard?

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:50.560
Screen?

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:52.260
What about the rest of it?

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:53.390
Flat?

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:54.520
Shiny?

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:55.650
Aluminum?

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:56.780
Why?”

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:59.970
Because you want to act like that’s one
thing.

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:04.230
You don’t want to act like it’s – “how
many transistors are on a chip now?

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:06.860
Like a billion or more?”

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:10.030
A lot, and they’re down to molecular size
those things.

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:15.390
They’re so small now, this is really funny,
they’re so small now that – so imagine

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:17.670
electrons running along a wire.

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:21.170
Now the weird thing about an electron is it
might be there and it might be somewhere else.

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:26.410
So it’s got this vague, non-locality about
it.

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:29.280
You know like maybe it’s supposed to be
there but it’s actually over there; and

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:31.170
would happen now and then.

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:35.720
But the wires are so damn thin now that there’s
some good probability that the electron will

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:38.260
be outside the wire, and that causes short-circuits.

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:44.670
So, that’s quite, well you’ve got quantum
level complexity mucking up your circuitry

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:45.819
in your computer.

00:03:45.819 --> 00:03:48.220
But look, the computer is designed this way,
right?

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:51.310
It’s designed so you can ignore it.

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:53.270
The outside of it is a flat shell.

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:56.140
It’s a low-resolution image.

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:59.900
And, not the keyboard because you have to
move it, not the screen and everything else

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:01.340
is flat.

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:05.760
The designer, the primate designer of this
computer does not want your poor primate brain

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:10.160
to be overwhelmed by the fact that this thing
is incomprehensibly complex.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:13.790
Cars are the same they have that nice shiny
shell on them and so that you can just sort

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:16.709
of low resolution it and that’s good enough.

00:04:16.709 --> 00:04:22.140
The walls painted roughly the same color,
we’re doing a lot things around us to reduce

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:23.669
the sensory lobe.

00:04:23.669 --> 00:04:28.410
Plus, there are people beadling away in the
background like mad making sure the lights

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:32.410
stay on and making sure the heat stays on,
you know making sure you have air in here.

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:35.760
The place isn’t burning down, there aren’t
crazy people wandering around in here with

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:37.450
shotguns and rifles.

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:42.820
There’s a lot going on so that you can sit
here and pretend that you know enough to stay

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:43.820
alive.

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:50.840
So, we’re also removing a tremendous amount
of complexity as a consequence of social cooperation.

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:56.900
Okay, so these are all parts of the way that
you simplify the world.

00:04:56.900 --> 00:05:00.970
You’re in a radical simplification structure
that’s the external society.

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:04.160
Then within that there’s only certain things
you want, and then within that there’s only

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:10.500
certain things you can detect, and then within
that there’s only certain features that

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:15.840
pop out as necessary to contemplate in the
realm that you’re dealing with.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:20.240
Now, one thing you might ask is well, “what
time span are you usually dealing with?”

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:23.930
You know cause what the world’s made of
depends on the time span; and that’s a tough

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:24.930
one.

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:26.680
We know that it’s more than 1/10th of a
second.

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:29.770
But you can think years into the future, right?

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:32.160
So I might say well, “why are you going
to university?”

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:35.890
Or I might say, “what are you doing right
now?”

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:39.600
And then the answer would be, well I’m sitting
in class.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:41.660
And then the question would be, “well why
are you sitting in class?”

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:45.780
And the answer to that would be well because
I want to take this course.

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:46.780
“And why?”

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:49.480
Well it’s because I want to get my degree.

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:50.480
“Why?”

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:54.830
Well, because I want to be prepared for a
career and, you know, a productive and high

00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:56.630
quality life when I get out.

00:05:56.630 --> 00:05:57.630
Well, “why?”

00:05:57.630 --> 00:06:02.820
Well you know you can keep saying why, pretty
much indefinitely and as you answer you go

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:05.139
farther and farther out in time scales.

00:06:05.139 --> 00:06:09.590
Now, it’s a tricky thing because one of
the things I might tell you, and this is a

00:06:09.590 --> 00:06:14.850
very complex cognitive problems is, “well
where should you stop?”

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:20.700
So for example, I could say, “do you want
100 dollars now or do you want 50 000 years

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:21.920
in 75 years?”

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:25.710
You’re going to think, “well I’ll probably
take the 100 dollars now.”

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:27.370
“Well how about in 200 years?”

00:06:27.370 --> 00:06:30.750
It’s like, well I’m not going to be there
I’ll take the 100 bucks now.

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:33.760
So obviously thinking out 200 years is not
that helpful.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:39.510
Now you might think well no, you should calculate
the effects of your actions on the vastest

00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:45.060
time span possible and include the largest
number of people, if you’re really going

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:46.540
to equilibrate it.

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:48.790
But the problem is you can’t do the computations.

00:06:48.790 --> 00:06:54.370
Because you get, you get this called combinatorial
explosion happening, which is that, it’s

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:57.290
like a chessboard.

00:06:57.290 --> 00:06:58.830
You make one move, okay fine.

00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:02.180
But then you could make four and with each
of those you could make four more and then

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:05.000
with each of those you could make four more
and soon if you’re looking twenty moves

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:08.430
ahead, it’s like there’s so many moves
that the other person could possibly make,

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:10.460
that looking that far ahead is pointless.

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:12.699
You can’t do the computations.

00:07:12.699 --> 00:07:16.970
And so, we don’t know how far you should
look into the future, it depends on how stable

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:19.180
your environment is.

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:24.990
But, one month seems okay, a year, yeah, three
to five years, that’s what Ill have you

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:27.470
do when I have you do the future-authoring
program.

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:29.949
“Twenty years?”

00:07:29.949 --> 00:07:32.190
Hmm, probably not.

00:07:32.190 --> 00:07:36.540
And the reason for that is the margin of error
in your prediction grows so large that your

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:39.830
prediction isn’t worth anything, and you
can really see how that’s the case now,

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:43.070
cause like, “what’s it going to be like
in twenty years?”

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:44.740
When I was a kid I used to read science fiction.

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:48.150
It was like, plausible accounts of the potential
future.

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:51.460
It’s like, now, who knows?

00:07:51.460 --> 00:07:53.240
Anything could happen.

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:54.240
Anything could happen.

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:56.550
You know you could be three quarters robot
in twenty years.

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:58.449
You could have a lifespan of ten thousand
years.

00:07:58.449 --> 00:08:00.430
I mean, we just do not know.

00:08:00.430 --> 00:08:03.080
We could be living in caves again with everything
in ruins.

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:05.430
We don’t have a clue what’s going to happen.

00:08:05.430 --> 00:08:10.210
So twenty-year span, I would say, get friendly
with computers.

00:08:10.210 --> 00:08:12.120
So twenty-year span is too long.

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:18.270
So, okay, anyways what I ‘m pointing out
is that you have to calculate, you have to

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:23.979
calculate what you’re doing in across multiple
spans of time, and in the combination, and

00:08:23.979 --> 00:08:26.180
in combination with many many people.

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:27.669
And that makes things very complex.

00:08:27.669 --> 00:08:31.310
But that’s how you’re building the structures
that you live within.

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:34.289
Alright, so back to this.

00:08:34.289 --> 00:08:39.949
You’re at point A you’re going to point
B. So, you are somewhere.

00:08:39.949 --> 00:08:42.019
Now, that’s interesting.

00:08:42.019 --> 00:08:43.440
You are somewhere.

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:46.519
Now generally what people think about when
they think about being somewhere is that they’re

00:08:46.519 --> 00:08:47.519
in a place.

00:08:47.519 --> 00:08:49.249
But you’re not just in a place.

00:08:49.249 --> 00:08:52.350
You’re in a place at a time.

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:56.550
And I really want to stress that because we
know that the universe, we know that the environment

00:08:56.550 --> 00:08:58.740
is four-dimensional, not three-dimensional.

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:05.829
That’s important because you could be in
the same place, and in the next second you’re

00:09:05.829 --> 00:09:08.939
still where you were but you’re in a completely
different place.

00:09:08.939 --> 00:09:14.670
So for example, if someone walked in here
with a pistol, this would not be the same

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:15.670
place.

00:09:15.670 --> 00:09:18.920
Okay, so there’s a reason I’m telling
you that.

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:24.259
And then, there’s the future, and that’s
also a place in time and a place in space.

00:09:24.259 --> 00:09:30.209
Okay, now you’re getting from point to the
other, and you’re going to do that by implementing

00:09:30.209 --> 00:09:31.209
certain behaviors.

00:09:31.209 --> 00:09:34.480
Now you might say, what can happen while your
doing that?

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:36.629
Well here’s three things that can happen.

00:09:36.629 --> 00:09:41.980
The thing you want, that can happen.

00:09:41.980 --> 00:09:47.819
Something you don’t want, but that you understand,
and something you don’t understand at all,

00:09:47.819 --> 00:09:49.420
something completely beyond your comprehension.

00:09:49.420 --> 00:09:53.129
And that would generally be a traumatizing
event of some sort.

00:09:53.129 --> 00:09:55.550
So, you can either know what’s going to
happen.

00:09:55.550 --> 00:09:59.809
Or you cannot know and still be able to cope
with it, or you can not know and not cope

00:09:59.809 --> 00:10:01.300
with it.

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:04.480
Of the negative ones they correspond to that
hierarchy, right?

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:09.410
If it’s a little thing that you don’t
understand you just modify a little behavior.

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:14.379
If it’s a big thing that you don’t understand,
well then what?

00:10:14.379 --> 00:10:15.759
Well then everything falls apart.

00:10:15.759 --> 00:10:20.759
Don’t know where you are, don’t know where
you’re going, and all the behaviors you

00:10:20.759 --> 00:10:22.350
have are not helpful.

00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:24.489
And that’s when your world falls apart.

00:10:24.489 --> 00:10:27.769
So, generally that doesn’t happen.

00:10:27.769 --> 00:10:30.649
So, let’s say I set my target on the door.

00:10:30.649 --> 00:10:31.959
I’m going to walk to the door.

00:10:31.959 --> 00:10:34.209
So here’s what happens.

00:10:34.209 --> 00:10:39.610
Instantly, I’m slightly, I have slight negative
emotion about the fact that any of blank issue

00:10:39.610 --> 00:10:40.990
in the way people exist.

00:10:40.990 --> 00:10:46.819
Because what my brain is doing is reconfiguring
the world, to chart a straight path and anything

00:10:46.819 --> 00:10:52.170
that’s in the way is going to make me uneasy-
frustrated, disappointed, anxious.

00:10:52.170 --> 00:10:54.980
And you can think about this when you do house
design, by the way.

00:10:54.980 --> 00:10:58.550
So, when you’re setting up a room, what
you want to do is figure out what the room

00:10:58.550 --> 00:10:59.550
is for.

00:10:59.550 --> 00:11:01.610
Then you want to clear the paths for that.

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:06.820
Because then when you walk into the room,
the room will say, “Welcome!

00:11:06.820 --> 00:11:09.680
You can do the things you want to do here,
easily.”

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:12.509
And if you don’t do that, it’s all cluttered
up.

00:11:12.509 --> 00:11:15.930
It’s like, there’s no difference between
that and being confused mentally.

00:11:15.930 --> 00:11:17.790
It’s exactly the same thing.

00:11:17.790 --> 00:11:23.029
So, okay, so let’s say I now stand up and
that works and then I walk that way, and that

00:11:23.029 --> 00:11:24.029
way and that way.

00:11:24.029 --> 00:11:27.999
I keep my eyes on the door, and I’m getting
towards the door.

00:11:27.999 --> 00:11:33.990
Well I’m moderately positive about that,
because I’m getting where I’m going, but

00:11:33.990 --> 00:11:39.279
I’m also moderately positive about it because
the fact that I’m getting where I’m going

00:11:39.279 --> 00:11:41.920
validates my whole theory.

00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:44.760
So one of the things that the behaviorists
didn’t get right when they were thinking

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:50.439
about punishment - If an animal lays out a
behavior and you punish it, it’ll stop manifesting

00:11:50.439 --> 00:11:52.430
that behavior.

00:11:52.430 --> 00:11:54.949
But that isn’t all that happens.

00:11:54.949 --> 00:11:57.910
You think about punishing a human being.

00:11:57.910 --> 00:12:02.090
They stop manifesting the behavior, but the
behavior isn’t the whole damn thing.

00:12:02.090 --> 00:12:04.769
The thing you’re in is this, it’s the
whole thing.

00:12:04.769 --> 00:12:07.791
It’s the conception of where they are, it’s
the conception of where they’re going, and

00:12:07.791 --> 00:12:09.889
it’s all the behaviors they might need to
get there.

00:12:09.889 --> 00:12:13.959
So, you know, maybe they’ll try to circumvent
the punishment, walk around it.

00:12:13.959 --> 00:12:19.610
Maybe they’ll try a few more behaviors,
but what happens, you know, so I’m tied

00:12:19.610 --> 00:12:22.420
by a chain and I don’t know it, I’m tied
by a chain to the wall.

00:12:22.420 --> 00:12:25.220
I start walking that way and I figure out
that I’m chained.

00:12:25.220 --> 00:12:28.389
Well, so much for that stupid theory, I’m
not getting to the door.

00:12:28.389 --> 00:12:34.220
I’m going to let that view of the world
disintegrate, I’m going to be very upset

00:12:34.220 --> 00:12:38.589
about that, and then I have to come up with
another one, which might be, “well how do

00:12:38.589 --> 00:12:40.589
I get rid of the chain?”

00:12:40.589 --> 00:12:44.110
So when you fail, two things happen.

00:12:44.110 --> 00:12:48.429
The behavior that you’re manifesting doesn’t
work, that’s the behaviorist routine.

00:12:48.429 --> 00:12:54.410
But it’s worse than that because the whole
little personality that you’re using to

00:12:54.410 --> 00:12:58.350
construe the world falls apart.

00:12:58.350 --> 00:13:03.840
And the more important the thing is that has
fallen apart, the more of a catastrophe it’s

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:05.139
going to be for you.

00:13:05.139 --> 00:13:07.709
So let’s say you’re going to medical school,
that’s what you want to do.

00:13:07.709 --> 00:13:11.740
Alright, so, you’re taking the course to
write the exam to pass the course with an

00:13:11.740 --> 00:13:15.529
A to apply to medical school to get into medical
school to be a doctor.

00:13:15.529 --> 00:13:18.439
You fail the test, “what just happened?”

00:13:18.439 --> 00:13:22.339
“ Well, did your whole future fall apart?”

00:13:22.339 --> 00:13:25.029
“Or did you just fail the test?”

00:13:25.029 --> 00:13:26.610
Who knows, right?

00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:31.309
Maybe you’re whole future just fell apart,
it’s like, now you have a GPA of 3.3, like

00:13:31.309 --> 00:13:35.850
No, you have to go to the Caribbean to a private
medical school.

00:13:35.850 --> 00:13:39.410
You’re not going to get in anywhere in Canada
or the United States, it’s like, okay, “is

00:13:39.410 --> 00:13:40.410
your life over?”

00:13:40.410 --> 00:13:41.410
“Do you jump off a bridge?”

00:13:41.410 --> 00:13:43.110
Well sometimes people do.

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:47.579
And it’s because the little sub-personality
that they created which is the “me that

00:13:47.579 --> 00:13:51.910
will be a doctor”, it’s one of these things
the little story, it’s so integral to their

00:13:51.910 --> 00:13:56.709
entire identity, that when it collapses they’re
done.

00:13:56.709 --> 00:13:59.299
And people get suicidal when that sort of
thing happens.

00:13:59.299 --> 00:14:02.230
Okay, so well, “what are you supposed to
do when that happens?”

00:14:02.230 --> 00:14:04.739
Well, that’s something that we’ll talk
about.

00:14:04.739 --> 00:14:09.429
You know, from the perspective of mental hygiene,
one of the things I would recommend is that

00:14:09.429 --> 00:14:13.749
when things go wrong for you, you start at
the highest level of resolution in your hierarchy

00:14:13.749 --> 00:14:14.749
and work upward.

00:14:14.749 --> 00:14:17.959
You don’t say, “well God, now everything’s
over!”

00:14:17.959 --> 00:14:23.429
You know, you might say, “okay, what can
I do to ensure that I do better on the next

00:14:23.429 --> 00:14:24.429
test?”

00:14:24.429 --> 00:14:29.769
So, then you could analyze your subroutines,
your micro-subroutines, like how much do you

00:14:29.769 --> 00:14:31.339
study a day?

00:14:31.339 --> 00:14:34.180
Well, sometimes people will tell me “I study
six hours a day.”

00:14:34.180 --> 00:14:36.009
It’s like, no you don’t.

00:14:36.009 --> 00:14:39.540
You sit in front of what you’re supposed
to be doing for six hours a day, but if you

00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:42.110
actually study for more than three I would
be shocked.

00:14:42.110 --> 00:14:48.829
Cause I’ve met almost no one in my entire
life, who can sustain high-level concentration

00:14:48.829 --> 00:14:53.670
for more than 3 hours, for more than 6 days
a week for more than a year.

00:14:53.670 --> 00:14:55.370
You’ll just burn yourself to a crisp.

00:14:55.370 --> 00:14:58.359
So no, you’re deluding to yourself into
thinking that you’re studying six hours

00:14:58.359 --> 00:14:59.359
a day.

00:14:59.359 --> 00:15:03.220
You’d be way better off to cut it down to
two and a half and actually do it.

00:15:03.220 --> 00:15:05.990
There’s micro-routine analysis that you
can do.

00:15:05.990 --> 00:15:09.910
So that might be helpful, then you can implement
that and run it, that’s what you’d do

00:15:09.910 --> 00:15:11.139
if you’re a behavioral psychologist.

00:15:11.139 --> 00:15:14.569
Someone comes and says, “my life’s over”
and you say, “well let’s go through the

00:15:14.569 --> 00:15:15.600
micro-routines.”

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:16.600
Well, they say, “well what do you mean?

00:15:16.600 --> 00:15:18.329
Well they say, “I can’t be a doctor.”

00:15:18.329 --> 00:15:19.329
Well, okay.

00:15:19.329 --> 00:15:20.980
“Could you be a nurse?

00:15:20.980 --> 00:15:22.559
Would that work?

00:15:22.559 --> 00:15:26.250
Could you be a medical technician?”

00:15:26.250 --> 00:15:31.239
You know, could you be a social worker?

00:15:31.239 --> 00:15:36.899
Etc, like okay so your domain is helpful medical
type of person.

00:15:36.899 --> 00:15:42.209
Well one element of that is doctor, but it
doesn’t exhaust the entire realm of possibilities.

00:15:42.209 --> 00:15:44.759
So I’d say flex up a bit!

00:15:44.759 --> 00:15:47.610
You know, it’s not the only path.

00:15:47.610 --> 00:15:51.119
And so that would be a more high-level adjustment,
that’d be a more cognitive adjustment.

00:15:51.119 --> 00:15:54.749
If you’re in Cognitive Behavioral Therapy,
it’s like, let’s check out your subroutines,

00:15:54.749 --> 00:15:59.989
make you more effective at studying, but you
know, let’s loosen up your top-level abstractions.

00:15:59.989 --> 00:16:02.110
You should be a good person, let’s say.

00:16:02.110 --> 00:16:03.889
Whatever you decide that means.

00:16:03.889 --> 00:16:08.689
One element of that is, be productively employed
in a way that doesn’t make you bitter and

00:16:08.689 --> 00:16:09.689
nasty.

00:16:09.689 --> 00:16:14.089
Okay, doctor might be one way of solving that,
although I wouldn’t be so sure of that.

00:16:14.089 --> 00:16:17.899
But it’s not the only way of solving it.

00:16:17.899 --> 00:16:26.379
So, alright, you go from point A to B. You
go from point A to point B, and things can

00:16:26.379 --> 00:16:27.550
happen along the way.

00:16:27.550 --> 00:16:32.730
Things you like – positive emotion, you’re
getting there, plus you’re right.

00:16:32.730 --> 00:16:36.819
Things that produce negative emotion – you’re
not getting there, okay.

00:16:36.819 --> 00:16:40.579
You can either solve them easily, with small
behavioral adjustment, or you have to go up

00:16:40.579 --> 00:16:47.609
the hierarchy of value and adjust it with
the least possible damage, generally speaking-

00:16:47.609 --> 00:16:52.899
because that’s another rule of thumb in
therapy.

00:16:52.899 --> 00:16:56.399
I can’t be a doctor so I’m going to be
an engineer.

00:16:56.399 --> 00:16:58.100
Well, no.

00:16:58.100 --> 00:17:03.379
You’ve got three years behind you, don’t
just throw it away, use what you’ve got

00:17:03.379 --> 00:17:06.890
and make a minor course deviation, if you
can do it.

00:17:06.890 --> 00:17:10.910
And you know we can talk about broader scale
transformations, but they’re costly.

00:17:10.910 --> 00:17:15.079
But you know stick as close to the path as
you can, while altering it enough so that

00:17:15.079 --> 00:17:23.160
you can proceed to your final goal.

00:17:23.160 --> 00:17:26.920
And so, well I think this is where we’ll
stop today, that leads us to the next part

00:17:26.920 --> 00:17:27.920
of the story.

00:17:27.920 --> 00:17:30.730
So, here’s the kind of story that someone
can tell.

00:17:30.730 --> 00:17:33.340
I was at point A and I went to point B and
here’s how I did it.

00:17:33.340 --> 00:17:35.350
And you’re pretty happy about that story.

00:17:35.350 --> 00:17:40.520
Maybe you go on Youtube and, I found this
a year ago, I had this old car wanted to put

00:17:40.520 --> 00:17:45.200
a stereo in it and so I typed in, “how to
put a stereo in an x old car” and there’s

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:51.080
a helpful retired southern guy who, you know,
made a Youtube video out of it.

00:17:51.080 --> 00:17:56.860
I was interested in his little story, and
he didn’t have a car stereo in his car and

00:17:56.860 --> 00:18:00.740
he wanted one, I didn’t have a car stereo
in my car and I wanted one.

00:18:00.740 --> 00:18:04.010
So his little story about how to do that was
very interesting to me.

00:18:04.010 --> 00:18:07.540
So I ran the little tape and I did it.

00:18:07.540 --> 00:18:13.940
Great, not particularly transformational,
but good enough.

00:18:13.940 --> 00:18:15.440
I got some information.

00:18:15.440 --> 00:18:19.790
Alright, so that’s an alright story and
you’re running around all of the time interfacing

00:18:19.790 --> 00:18:22.840
with other people and you’re telling them
you’re little story and they’re telling

00:18:22.840 --> 00:18:24.800
you their little story.

00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:28.650
But you know so that’s one thing that interests
people – how to do something successfully.

00:18:28.650 --> 00:18:31.460
But a lot of what people talk about is something
different.

00:18:31.460 --> 00:18:35.220
Which is, I was trying to get from point A
to B and it didn’t work and now I’m at

00:18:35.220 --> 00:18:36.900
a place where it didn’t work.

00:18:36.900 --> 00:18:39.330
And so it’s a problem, right?

00:18:39.330 --> 00:18:42.590
The world’s opened up around me, I don’t
know what to do.

00:18:42.590 --> 00:18:46.340
And the reason you talk to people about that,
is because they have brains.

00:18:46.340 --> 00:18:47.340
And so do you.

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:49.770
And you might as well have two brains working
if you can and if you’re reciprocal, you

00:18:49.770 --> 00:18:55.550
know, you’re in trouble you come to me,
and we talk it out, and maybe I can help.

00:18:55.550 --> 00:19:03.480
And then because we’re playing a interitive
game, now you owe me a favor.

00:19:03.480 --> 00:19:09.570
Now, you know, I’m not going to be too restrictive
about that, but you know, socially intelligent

00:19:09.570 --> 00:19:12.070
people are reciprocal.

00:19:12.070 --> 00:19:16.830
Ben Franklin once said, you’re new in a
neighborhood what should you do to get integrated?

00:19:16.830 --> 00:19:19.320
He said, ask a neighbor for a favor.

00:19:19.320 --> 00:19:22.970
A small favor – don’t ask will you build
me a new house?

00:19:22.970 --> 00:19:27.150
It’s like, no that’s not going to work.

00:19:27.150 --> 00:19:31.190
But, “my dog’s on the front lawn would
you watch him for 2 minutes while I run to

00:19:31.190 --> 00:19:32.350
the corner store?”

00:19:32.350 --> 00:19:33.660
It’s like, okay they can do that.

00:19:33.660 --> 00:19:34.760
Well why would you do that?

00:19:34.760 --> 00:19:37.390
Well it opens up the possibility of a reciprocal
exchange.

00:19:37.390 --> 00:19:41.150
It’s very very smart, cause now they can
ask you for a favor.

00:19:41.150 --> 00:19:43.660
So you get the trade thing going.

00:19:43.660 --> 00:19:48.660
People who are highly social are really good
at identifying and maintaining reciprocal

00:19:48.660 --> 00:19:49.660
obligations.

00:19:49.660 --> 00:19:53.410
So, we use each other as problem solving resources.

00:19:53.410 --> 00:19:55.450
And a lot of our communication is exactly
that.

00:19:55.450 --> 00:20:00.270
And so basically the story you’re telling
isn’t I was at point A and I went to point

00:20:00.270 --> 00:20:01.270
B and everything worked.

00:20:01.270 --> 00:20:04.290
The story is this instead.

00:20:04.290 --> 00:20:11.220
It’s half of this.

00:20:11.220 --> 00:20:14.490
I was at point A (that’s the top left hand
corner), I was at point A and I was going

00:20:14.490 --> 00:20:17.770
to point B but something didn’t work.

00:20:17.770 --> 00:20:22.500
And then things collapsed, you know, to a
greater or lesser extent, depending on the

00:20:22.500 --> 00:20:27.320
severity of the event, and the closeness of
your friendships that you’re telling the

00:20:27.320 --> 00:20:31.060
story, you’re not going to tell catastrophic
stories to someone on a bus that you just

00:20:31.060 --> 00:20:33.360
sat down beside, unless you have a mental
illness.

00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:36.490
Well, really it’s the truth.

00:20:36.490 --> 00:20:40.430
Sometimes you sit down on a bus, and someone
who has a mental illness will come and sit

00:20:40.430 --> 00:20:44.310
beside you and they will immediately tell
you horrible things about their life.

00:20:44.310 --> 00:20:46.320
And the reason for that is, they have to.

00:20:46.320 --> 00:20:49.710
They don’t have anyone to talk to, they
can’t sort out their brains.

00:20:49.710 --> 00:20:54.390
They’re so desperate, they’re so ruined,
they’re in chaos.

00:20:54.390 --> 00:21:03.560
They’re so ruined, that the proclivity to
proclaim the chaos absolutely overwhelms them.

00:21:03.560 --> 00:21:07.110
And you just can’t deal with someone like
that most of the time because the magnitude

00:21:07.110 --> 00:21:11.530
of the problem they’re facing you with is
way beyond your capacity to solve, so it’s

00:21:11.530 --> 00:21:12.530
just awkward.

00:21:12.530 --> 00:21:13.530
It’s not good.

00:21:13.530 --> 00:21:16.450
Anyways, you say, I was going from point A
to point B, didn’t work!

00:21:16.450 --> 00:21:22.150
Now I don’t know where I am, I don’t know
where I’m going and I’m lost.

00:21:22.150 --> 00:21:25.433
And you say, “in my experience when I was
in a situation like this, I did this, I did

00:21:25.433 --> 00:21:30.230
this and I know someone else in that situation
who did this maybe you could try those.”

00:21:30.230 --> 00:21:33.810
Or maybe you just shut up, and you let the
person talk!

00:21:33.810 --> 00:21:35.550
And that’s often extremely useful.

00:21:35.550 --> 00:21:38.330
And the reason you do that is because they’re
thinking.

00:21:38.330 --> 00:21:41.500
Because people think they think, but they
don’t.

00:21:41.500 --> 00:21:43.390
they talk.

00:21:43.390 --> 00:21:44.390
Thinking is hard.

00:21:44.390 --> 00:21:49.160
Right, it’s a very complex abstract, cognitive
operation.

00:21:49.160 --> 00:21:53.440
And I don’t think that they’re many more
people who can think, than there are people

00:21:53.440 --> 00:21:54.990
who can really read.

00:21:54.990 --> 00:21:58.730
And that’s probably 2-5% of the population
at most.

00:21:58.730 --> 00:22:01.720
And so most of the way that people deal with
that is that they talk.

00:22:01.720 --> 00:22:06.220
And that’s their thinking, and so they have
some ideas about what might be going on, but

00:22:06.220 --> 00:22:11.650
they’re mixed and muddy, so they’re basically
throwing out these ideas, “did this happen?

00:22:11.650 --> 00:22:12.650
Is he like this?

00:22:12.650 --> 00:22:13.760
Is she like that?

00:22:13.760 --> 00:22:15.370
Did I do this?”

00:22:15.370 --> 00:22:19.760
And then they watch you, and if you’re nodding
about one then they think, oh well, you know,

00:22:19.760 --> 00:22:23.640
that seems plausible, and they you lay out
another, and they don’t even notice and

00:22:23.640 --> 00:22:26.680
then you think well that’s off the table,
I didn’t think that was a very good idea,

00:22:26.680 --> 00:22:33.110
but now I’ve got some social validation,
and soon they talk it out, and instead of

00:22:33.110 --> 00:22:39.370
having sixty vaguely defined problems, now
they have five very tightly defined problems.

00:22:39.370 --> 00:22:42.270
They’re feeling a lot better about that,
right, because it’s five snakes instead

00:22:42.270 --> 00:22:43.270
of sixty.

00:22:43.270 --> 00:22:45.310
And maybe they can deal with five snakes.

00:22:45.310 --> 00:22:48.500
So you’ve already helped them a whole bunch
just by listening.

00:22:48.500 --> 00:22:52.880
You know, and then maybe you can be an active
listener, they lay out their story and you

00:22:52.880 --> 00:22:56.480
say, well you said something here that doesn’t
make any sense with this thing that you said.

00:22:56.480 --> 00:22:58.980
So you know, how would you reconcile those?

00:22:58.980 --> 00:23:04.000
You know, it’s this dialectical process
and so the person collapses into chaos, and

00:23:04.000 --> 00:23:08.350
they come and tell you about it and you rescue
them if you can out of the chaos and then

00:23:08.350 --> 00:23:11.550
they pop back up the other side.

00:23:11.550 --> 00:23:16.770
Clinical practice is actually a formalized
version of an ideal relationship.

00:23:16.770 --> 00:23:19.440
So you think, “what’s an ideal relationship?”

00:23:19.440 --> 00:23:24.340
Well, I would say that an ideal relationship
is where both of you are trying to get better

00:23:24.340 --> 00:23:26.620
than you are and you’re helping each other
do it.

00:23:26.620 --> 00:23:29.420
It’s something like that.

00:23:29.420 --> 00:23:32.260
You know if I have a good relationship with
you I would rather that you’re the best

00:23:32.260 --> 00:23:33.760
you, you can be.

00:23:33.760 --> 00:23:38.110
And I would assume that you would also like
that for me.

00:23:38.110 --> 00:23:42.720
I mean things interfere with that because
you might say, well if given how horrible

00:23:42.720 --> 00:23:47.810
I am, if my partner all of a sudden got a
clue, they’d leave, so I’d better interfere

00:23:47.810 --> 00:23:49.800
with that as much as I possibly can.

00:23:49.800 --> 00:23:51.100
People do that all the time.

00:23:51.100 --> 00:23:54.230
You know one of the things they’ll do is
make sure their partner isn’t too attractive.

00:23:54.230 --> 00:23:57.280
It’s like, if you’re so attractive, why
are you with someone like me?

00:23:57.280 --> 00:24:00.560
It’s like, I’ll criticize you when you
look attractive and that’ll fix that.

00:24:00.560 --> 00:24:03.410
So, and people do that sort of thing all the
time.

00:24:03.410 --> 00:24:06.030
This is the revolutionary story.

00:24:06.030 --> 00:24:08.610
This is the real story people tell.

00:24:08.610 --> 00:24:13.900
I was here and I got bloody-well knocked off
my perch and then for a while things were

00:24:13.900 --> 00:24:14.900
not good.

00:24:14.900 --> 00:24:19.750
And then you know I reconfigured myself, I
learned some things, I did some exploration,

00:24:19.750 --> 00:24:21.340
I made some changes, and poof!

00:24:21.340 --> 00:24:22.980
Off the other side I come.

00:24:22.980 --> 00:24:25.010
And I’m a better person for it.

00:24:25.010 --> 00:24:30.020
And if you listen to the way people talk about
their pasts, especially with regards to traumatic

00:24:30.020 --> 00:24:31.180
events, they’ll often tell you that.

00:24:31.180 --> 00:24:33.880
They’ll say, well you know in retrospect
that was the best thing that ever happened

00:24:33.880 --> 00:24:34.880
to me.

00:24:34.880 --> 00:24:37.750
It’s like, those are the people who come
out the other side I’ll tell you.

00:24:37.750 --> 00:24:43.350
Lots of people, they fall into the belly of
the beast and they never come out.

00:24:43.350 --> 00:24:45.040
You see that fairy tales some times.

00:24:45.040 --> 00:24:49.160
So there will be a hero, and he’ll conquer
some sort of dragon or some sort of monster

00:24:49.160 --> 00:24:51.190
and when he cuts it open and a bunch of people
come out.

00:24:51.190 --> 00:24:52.360
It’s like, yeah!

00:24:52.360 --> 00:24:54.650
They’re the ones that got swallowed by the
thing.

00:24:54.650 --> 00:24:57.820
And it isn’t until that person comes along
that solves the problem that all those other

00:24:57.820 --> 00:24:59.640
people get to come out.

00:24:59.640 --> 00:25:02.190
So, that’s a revolutionary story.

00:25:02.190 --> 00:25:04.420
It’s the archetypal revolutionary story.

00:25:04.420 --> 00:25:06.870
And it’s the story of human progress.

00:25:06.870 --> 00:25:11.470
Socially, politically, economically, psychologically.

00:25:11.470 --> 00:25:15.320
And you see that reflected, for example, one
of the things I could tell you about Christianity

00:25:15.320 --> 00:25:17.660
is that it’s a revolutionary narrative.

00:25:17.660 --> 00:25:21.951
At the beginning, so if you look at the Bible,
structured like a piece of literature, at

00:25:21.951 --> 00:25:26.310
the beginning there’s a fall, everyone was
doing fine and then they wake up and they

00:25:26.310 --> 00:25:29.380
find out that the bottom is dropped out.

00:25:29.380 --> 00:25:34.300
And then the whole rest of this book is about,
okay, how do you get back up?

00:25:34.300 --> 00:25:39.970
Actually it is in this, I’ll tell you, partly
what the Bible is, is the attempt by, broadly

00:25:39.970 --> 00:25:44.990
speaking Western civilization, so that would
be all the way from the Middle East, to the

00:25:44.990 --> 00:25:49.540
west - it’s the West’s attempt to figure
out what’s wrong with life and how to fix

00:25:49.540 --> 00:25:50.620
it.

00:25:50.620 --> 00:25:56.220
And it’s the collaboration of thousands
of people over thousands of years, of course

00:25:56.220 --> 00:26:00.200
you can say that about any ancient moral tradition.

00:26:00.200 --> 00:26:03.590
So, the question is, “well is there an answer
to that question?”

00:26:03.590 --> 00:26:11.220
Well that’s one of the next things we’re
going to try to figure out.

00:26:11.220 --> 00:26:14.410
Well let’s say you’re driving down the
401.

00:26:14.410 --> 00:26:15.450
Your car breaks down.

00:26:15.450 --> 00:26:20.170
You’re off by the side of the road, maybe
it’s snowing, it’s like “how bad is

00:26:20.170 --> 00:26:21.170
that?”

00:26:21.170 --> 00:26:22.260
Well you don’t know.

00:26:22.260 --> 00:26:26.830
Maybe someone slides on the snow and runs
over you, you’re dead, it’s really bad.

00:26:26.830 --> 00:26:31.380
So you’re freaking out, maybe your phone
isn’t working so you check your phone, that’s

00:26:31.380 --> 00:26:33.960
not working, it’s like all of a sudden things
have gotten worse.

00:26:33.960 --> 00:26:36.320
So this person drives up behind you and they
stop.

00:26:36.320 --> 00:26:38.420
You’re a bit nervous about them because
what are they up to?

00:26:38.420 --> 00:26:42.180
And they come up, they open the hood and they
say, “well this thing’s loose.”

00:26:42.180 --> 00:26:43.180
And they put it in there.

00:26:43.180 --> 00:26:44.460
It’s like, are you happy?

00:26:44.460 --> 00:26:48.590
Well, yes, because what’s happened to you
is magic.

00:26:48.590 --> 00:26:51.480
First of all you’re not in a car, soon as
it stops working by the way.

00:26:51.480 --> 00:26:54.170
It’s only a car when it’s working.

00:26:54.170 --> 00:26:56.760
When it’s not working it’s a bag of snakes,
right?

00:26:56.760 --> 00:26:59.200
It’s like, uh oh, this car isn’t working.

00:26:59.200 --> 00:27:00.990
“What could be wrong with it?”

00:27:00.990 --> 00:27:01.990
OH!

00:27:01.990 --> 00:27:04.680
It’s an infinite number of things, none
of which I understand!

00:27:04.680 --> 00:27:07.770
And those things are all attached to people
who are going to fix the car.

00:27:07.770 --> 00:27:09.350
“Can I trust them?

00:27:09.350 --> 00:27:10.350
Do I even know who they are?

00:27:10.350 --> 00:27:12.000
Are they going to empty my wallet?

00:27:12.000 --> 00:27:14.620
Do I need to have a new car?”

00:27:14.620 --> 00:27:18.420
So you don’t know, and then well your day
is gone.

00:27:18.420 --> 00:27:23.320
So all of a sudden, what happens is you’re
environment complexifies radically.

00:27:23.320 --> 00:27:27.430
And you get all nervous because there’s
a lot of paths in there that you might have

00:27:27.430 --> 00:27:29.650
to traverse to get back to where you’re
going.

00:27:29.650 --> 00:27:31.100
Someone comes along and goes, tap!

00:27:31.100 --> 00:27:34.180
It’s like you’re happy, and all the complexities
collapsed.

00:27:34.180 --> 00:27:41.200
In one path it went poof, your story is now
implementable again.

00:27:41.200 --> 00:27:42.520
And you’ll pay people for that.

00:27:42.520 --> 00:27:43.520
You do it all the time.

00:27:43.520 --> 00:27:45.620
It’s like, you got a problem, you take it
to an expert.

00:27:45.620 --> 00:27:50.580
They say, it’ll take you an infinite amount
of time to traverse all these possible paths

00:27:50.580 --> 00:27:53.590
so you’re basically screwed, but if you
give me a hundred dollars I can tell you how

00:27:53.590 --> 00:27:55.800
to do it in five minutes.

00:27:55.800 --> 00:27:57.070
You think, “yes!

00:27:57.070 --> 00:27:58.350
That’s worth it!”

00:27:58.350 --> 00:28:01.760
So of course it’s efficient, that’s why
we trade.

00:28:01.760 --> 00:28:04.809
It’s exactly why we trade.

00:28:04.809 --> 00:28:09.250
Things are going to go wrong with you that
I could help, and vice versa, what a deal!

00:28:09.250 --> 00:28:11.990
That’s partly why we’re social and why
we maintain it.

00:28:11.990 --> 00:28:18.360
Like as a collective, we’re way more intelligent
than we would be if we were one person.

00:28:18.360 --> 00:28:22.300
So, alright, that’s a revolutionary story.

00:28:22.300 --> 00:28:26.830
That’s a collapse into the underworld, and
a rebirth.

00:28:26.830 --> 00:28:30.620
And so as soon as you know that it’s so
cool to know that because now, whenever you

00:28:30.620 --> 00:28:34.610
read a classic story of any sort you can think
oh that’s a voyage to the underworld!

00:28:34.610 --> 00:28:37.040
I’ve been there, and you have!

00:28:37.040 --> 00:28:42.030
everyone’s been there, the underworld is
where that’s chaos it’s chaos, it’s

00:28:42.030 --> 00:28:44.270
where you go when your life falls apart.

00:28:44.270 --> 00:28:45.970
It has a structure.

00:28:45.970 --> 00:28:50.750
And what’s even cooler about that is, once
you know this, there’s actually guides through

00:28:50.750 --> 00:28:51.750
it.

00:28:51.750 --> 00:28:52.750
It’s like, okay, your world fell apart.

00:28:52.750 --> 00:28:55.770
“What do you do about it?”

00:28:55.770 --> 00:28:57.980
Dying of stress is a bad idea.

00:28:57.980 --> 00:28:59.980
You don’t have to do that.

00:28:59.980 --> 00:29:04.890
So I was talking to one of the students during
the break.

00:29:04.890 --> 00:29:08.680
We were talking about character, and I said,
well, the best thing you can do is build your

00:29:08.680 --> 00:29:09.680
character.

00:29:09.680 --> 00:29:10.680
Here’s a test of character.

00:29:10.680 --> 00:29:15.440
A family member is dying, okay, are you useful
or useless?

00:29:15.440 --> 00:29:17.680
That’s the first question.

00:29:17.680 --> 00:29:23.040
And then a deeper question is, do you make
it as good as it can be?

00:29:23.040 --> 00:29:26.410
Which isn’t going to be that good by the
way, or do you turn it into hell?

00:29:26.410 --> 00:29:29.890
Because you can, and lots of people do.

00:29:29.890 --> 00:29:32.870
So one of the tests of character is - okay,
there’s a crisis.

00:29:32.870 --> 00:29:34.430
Like really!

00:29:34.430 --> 00:29:37.200
“How are you going to respond?

00:29:37.200 --> 00:29:38.200
Are you going to be helpful?

00:29:38.200 --> 00:29:39.960
Or are you going to be useless?”

00:29:39.960 --> 00:29:41.710
Or ever worse, are you going to make it worse?

00:29:41.710 --> 00:29:47.380
I would say, helpful, and the thing that’s
so cool about human beings is that we can

00:29:47.380 --> 00:29:48.820
do that.

00:29:48.820 --> 00:29:53.810
People are so tough it’s insane; as long
as they put themselves in the position.

00:29:53.810 --> 00:29:56.420
My sister-in-law works as a palliative care
nurse.

00:29:56.420 --> 00:30:00.580
It’s like all she does is work with people
who are going to die in two weeks.

00:30:00.580 --> 00:30:05.580
She’s a happy person, partly because she
goes home and thinks, hey this isn’t a palliative

00:30:05.580 --> 00:30:07.240
care ward!

00:30:07.240 --> 00:30:09.530
Well really man!

00:30:09.530 --> 00:30:11.680
Things can be worse.

00:30:11.680 --> 00:30:14.350
And she knows that and she’s reminded of
it every day.

00:30:14.350 --> 00:30:16.880
That’s a person who does not take things
for granted.

